{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"DEPENDENCY CORRECTION: 7761 must precede 172e's completion. The order everyone (including the\nrunning handoff prompt) has been assuming — \"172e, then 7761\" — is BACKWARDS for the remaining work.\n\nWHY, verified in both plans and in source:\n\n172e's two outstanding ACs are:\n  - \"a test asserts code-review's verify step resolves the `standard` class rather than `cfg.model`\"\n  - \"a test asserts that with classes configured for a non-default provider, no verifier path\n     resolves a model belonging to a different provider\"\n\nNeither is satisfiable today, because the resolution funnel does not resolve class names:\n  - src/rebar/llm/config.py:362 — `resolve_model` is literally\n        return step or workflow or cfg.model\n    with NO call to resolve_model_string / load_class_slots.\n  - src/rebar/llm/workflow/gates/code-review.yaml:143-155 — the `verify` step declares prompt,\n    mode, output_schema and `with` (including temperature: 0) and has NO `model:` key at all, so it\n    falls through to cfg.model, the FRONTIER model.\n\nSo adding `model: standard` to that YAML step TODAY would pass the LITERAL STRING \"standard\" to the\nrunner. The wiring 172e needs does not exist yet.\n\nAND THAT WIRING IS 7761's, NOT 172e's. 7761's plan says so explicitly: \"Resolution happens at the\nONE funnel every step model already passes through: config.resolve_model(cfg, step=, workflow=)\n(config.py:347), today literally `return step or workflow or cfg.model`. If the resolved string is\none of trivial/standard/frontier, resolve it via model_classes.load_class_slots(); otherwise return\nit unchanged.\" Its ACs carry it too: \"a test asserts `model_ladder: [trivial, standard, frontier]`\nin a workflow resolves each rung through the class config, and that a ladder of literal ids still\nresolves to those ids unchanged.\"\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR SEQUENCING: 7761 lands the reserved-word resolution in resolve_model; 172e then\nadds `model: standard` to code-review.yaml's verify step and asserts the verifier paths. Doing 172e\nfirst would mean either duplicating 7761's funnel change or shipping a YAML key that resolves to a\nliteral string. d23e stays blocked by both, which is already recorded and remains correct.\n\nRECORDED AS A BLOCKING LINK (7761 blocks 172e) so the order cannot drift again.\n\nNOTE ON WHAT 172e DID DELIVER, so this is not read as a failed ticket: its plan-review and\ncompletion paths ARE done and green — _verifier_cfg resolves the standard class (change 1119,\nVerified +1) and the through-callers tests exist (change 1128, Verified +1). What remains is\nspecifically the CODE-REVIEW verifier path, which is the one the epic handoff called the most\nconsequential, since code review is the gate being cut over to Bedrock."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785541932122698001,"uuid":"99918c94-ca9f-4d24-8de6-85fdaf3ace69"}